WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.280
LUKMAN RAMSEY:
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.420
Great to see so many people
here late in the day.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.850
I know it's been a long
day for a lot of us.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.050
Hope it's gone
well for you guys.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.280
But a very interesting
talk today,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.220
focused on machine
learning in production

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.439
and how to architect for scale.

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:28.230
So I am a member of
the solutions architect

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.450
team at Google Cloud.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.960
My role is to basically help
Google Cloud customers use

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.480
machine learning
technology, and turn that

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.700
into solutions that
ultimately get published

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.110
on our cloud.google.com site.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.412
This is a very near
and dear topic to me.

00:00:44.412 --> 00:00:46.620
And by the way, I'm going
to be joined later on stage

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.953
by a couple of colleagues,
Drew [? Houghton ?] and Kevin

00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:51.930
[? Tsai. ?] So I'll be bringing
them up later to talk about

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.800
some interesting topics related
to ML and production and how

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.300
you scale it.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.869
So let's take a
look at the agenda.

00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:01.410
First, we're going
to talk about what

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.650
we mean by scale in the
context of machine learning,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.610
because there are some specific
aspects with respect to scaling

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.140
that apply to
machine learning that

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.700
don't apply to other domains.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.910
We're going to give a
very high-level overview

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.840
of architecture for machine
learning on GCP, different ways

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.440
that you can do it, some
components and services that we

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.474
would typically employ in
a ML production system.

00:01:25.474 --> 00:01:27.390
We're going to talk about
Cloud ML Engine, one

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.050
of the key services that we
have on Google Cloud for machine

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.280
learning in production
and elsewhere.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.550
We're going to talk about
how to use Kubernetes for ML,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.230
and that's where
my colleague Drew

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.047
is going to come up and give
you an overview of that.

00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:44.880
And then we're going
to focus and drill down

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.840
on scaling in the context
of training and prediction.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.360
So there's a lot of content
to get through here.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.000
I'm going to try to move fast so
that we get through all of it.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.380
So let's get started.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.250
So I'd like to start
with this quote

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.980
by everybody who has ever
tried, namely, that doing

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.630
ML in production is hard.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.150
And if you have tried it, you
probably are aware of this.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.670
So anyway, let's talk about
what do we mean by scale?

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.850
When we say ML production
scaling, architecting

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.390
for scale, what do we mean?

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:20.820
Well, there's a basic definition
for scale in architecture

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.120
in general on
cloud or elsewhere,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.160
that, you know,
as load increases,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.950
we want to maintain
availability and response time.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.380
I mean, that's kind of
a standard definition

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.360
for scalability that isn't
really specific to ML.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.200
We want those
characteristics to remain

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.770
within acceptable ranges,
right, so as the load increases

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.200
on Predict, then we
want to make sure

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.240
that the latency associated
with each prediction

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.250
stays reasonably
constant or doesn't

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.520
fall outside of a range
that would not work for us.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.400
But you know, and again, for
training machine learning,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.930
we care about things like
having the time to convergence,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.540
that it scales kind of in a
reasonable way with the amount

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.707
of data that we have, right?

00:03:04.707 --> 00:03:06.498
So that's something
that we don't typically

00:03:06.498 --> 00:03:08.610
have to concern ourselves
with when we're scaling

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.320
in other production contexts.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.930
And then for prediction,
we want to make sure

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.280
that as the model
complexity increases,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.750
that the latency associated
with each request also scales

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.000
appropriately, that
we don't hit some,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.520
you know, exponential
or some wall

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.940
that where suddenly as our
model gets too complex,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.200
our prediction latency
falls off the cliff.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.940
And it's even more
complicated than that

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.620
with machine
learning, because you

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:38.060
have to worry about
machine-specific hardware,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.370
machine-learning-specific
hardware, like DPUs and TPUs,

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:40.870
right?

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:43.330
You know, those introduce
different dynamics

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.040
into the scaling process.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.000
And furthermore, as you know,
if you're building machine

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.560
learning models,
we don't just care

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.340
about performance in
training or production,

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.890
but also about the process
of building machine learning

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:57.490
models, right?

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:58.594
That has to scale, too.

00:03:58.594 --> 00:04:00.010
In other words,
we know that we're

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:03.760
going to have to run hundreds,
maybe thousands of experiments,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.340
and we need a system
that allows us to do that

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.010
at scale in an efficient way.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.396
So that's also a
really important thing

00:04:11.396 --> 00:04:12.520
to pay attention to, right?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.260
You know, we need to
have a system that

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.220
allows us to not just scale
when we're in production,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.760
but scale while we're training
our model in the development

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.920
environment.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.080
All right, so let's talk
about the architecture

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.910
landscape for ML in
production as it exists today.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.900
This is a rapidly changing
environment, right,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.020
and it's going to be probably a
little bit of a different story

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:36.640
even six months from now.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.080
But as it exists
today, we have a set

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.260
of best practices and standard
templates or architectures

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.000
that we would use for deploying
ML in production on cloud.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.290
So I just want to start by
making the point that Google

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:57.040
has achieved a scale in the
deployment of production

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.190
machine learning that really
probably isn't duplicated

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.680
anywhere else, at any other
company in the world, right?

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.490
So we have proven that it's
possible to deploy machine

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:10.942
learning models in production at
scale on the order of thousands

00:05:10.942 --> 00:05:11.650
of models, right?

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.120
In fact, that graph shows--

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.920
it doesn't actually
go out to 2018,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.736
but we're now well over
5,000 models in production.

00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:20.110
Pipelines that
are in production,

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.750
doing machine learning, that are
powering a lot of the services

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.750
that you see over
there on the right.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:32.020
Obviously Search, but Drive,
Translate, Gmail, Maps,

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:33.580
YouTube, and so on.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.930
So machine learning is critical
to all of those services.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.100
And all of those services
are supported by machine

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.640
learning pipelines running in
Google's own internal cloud.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.280
So we figured out how to do it.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.540
We had to.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.040
Machine learning is really
core to Google's business.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.480
And this is one of
the keys, the secrets,

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.890
that we've developed to solve
that scaling problem internally

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:00.700
at Google.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:05.820
We published this paper last
year, so it's no secret.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.630
But we have a
common architecture

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:10.200
that we use to build and deploy
our machine learning models

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.240
at Google called TFX.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.220
I mean, lesson
number one from that

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.040
is that having a common
architecture that you use

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.610
to build and deploy
and serve your models

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.810
is critical, right, if you're
going to do it at scale.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.660
And so we realized
that for a long time.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:29.430
We built this TFX
common architecture

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.180
based on the learnings
of many, many years

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.820
of doing this in production.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.490
And the other point
that I want to make

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.830
about scaling in general--

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.415
this applies particularly
to Google Cloud,

00:06:42.415 --> 00:06:45.290
and we're going to
assume that in this talk,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.850
that we're really talking about
serverless architecture, right?

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:50.860
So it's going to be
a lot harder for you

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:54.100
to scale if you're worried
about individual servers

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.470
for your training
cluster or even your data

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.250
pre-processing pipeline.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:05.140
And GCP is explicitly designed
to leverage serverless

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:08.210
architecture for all
parts of this pipeline.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.810
So we're going to assume that
we're talking about serverless.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.510
That kind of takes a lot
of the stuff off the table

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:14.880
with respect to scaling.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.720
But serverless architecture is
going to be key for scaling.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.340
So this is a high-level
architecture diagram of TFX.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.510
It was present in the paper.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.970
There's actually a talk in
the TensorFlow Dev Summit

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:32.850
that you can go out and
find on YouTube that

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.160
talks about this architecture.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.095
Not all of this has
been open sourced.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.510
There are a few
components of TFX

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.910
that have been open
sourced, and we do plan,

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:47.510
and we have explicitly
announced as such,

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.590
on open sourcing
the entire stack,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.480
but it's not all done yet.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.320
In particular, we have model
evaluation and validation,

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.660
TF model analysis, it
was just open sourced.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:04.800
On the data transformation
side, there is TF Transform.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.559
We've open sourced a serving
component as well, TF Serving.

00:08:07.559 --> 00:08:08.850
We actually did that last year.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.141
So by the way, those components
are the same components

00:08:11.141 --> 00:08:15.637
that Google uses in production
for our own internal workloads.

00:08:15.637 --> 00:08:17.220
So if you use these
components, you're

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:20.760
basically running on
the same code as Google.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.760
But for each of these
architectural components

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.070
that you would use in a common
architecture for machine

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.740
learning in production,
there are GCP services

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.190
that correspond to those.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:32.130
I'm not going to go
through all of them

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:33.480
just in the interest of time.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:39.470
I do want to point out that--

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:43.669
yeah, so those are all the
services, the hexagons for GCP

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:45.503
that correspond to each
of those components.

00:08:45.503 --> 00:08:47.835
Like I said, I'm not going
to go through the whole thing

00:08:47.835 --> 00:08:48.830
in detail.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.950
I do want to point out, again,
some of the key components

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.270
that you would use--

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:56.979
not TFX-based, right
but services on GCP

00:08:56.979 --> 00:08:58.770
that you would use to
solve these problems.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:04.700
So for training, ML Engine
and GKE are basically

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.920
Kubernetes with Kubeflow
sitting on top of it,

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.680
are two of the best
answers we have for that.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.930
We're going to talk in more
detail about each of those

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.280
later.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:19.202
For tuning, ML Engine offers a
hyperparameter tuning service,

00:09:19.202 --> 00:09:20.910
and if you haven't
taken advantage of it,

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:21.670
you really should.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.580
It's one of the
key differentiators

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:27.750
for Google and for Google
Cloud in the realm of machine

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:28.360
learning.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:30.630
In fact, I'm not aware
of another cloud service

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.894
that offers a tuning
service like this.

00:09:32.894 --> 00:09:34.310
You know, this
means adjusting all

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:38.760
of the knobs on your ML models
to optimize their performance.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:43.922
Model evaluation and validation,
I mentioned TFX model analysis.

00:09:43.922 --> 00:09:45.630
It's very critical,
actually, so that you

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.731
can understand how your model
is performing across all

00:09:48.731 --> 00:09:50.980
of your data sets, and if
there's a slice of the data,

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:53.970
for example, that it's
not performing well on.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.450
It's also critical for things
like explainability in ML,

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:59.790
which I would love to talk
about more, but we won't.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.410
But you have to be
able to break down

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:02.868
different slices
of your input data

00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:05.820
in order to understand how
your model's really performing

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.410
in production.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.560
And then finally, as I
mentioned, for serving

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.110
we have TF Serving, which
again would typically

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.650
be deployed on Kubernetes
on GKE and ML Engine,

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:19.300
as answers to that.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:23.630
So let's talk a little bit
more about Cloud ML Engine.

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:26.460
Cloud ML Engine has
been out for a while.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:27.702
It's quite robust now.

00:10:27.702 --> 00:10:29.160
We have a lot of
customers using it

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.720
for all kinds of
different workloads.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.770
It's a platform as as a
service for TensorFlow,

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:38.010
but as you know, not
just TensorFlow anymore.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:41.130
So you can run xgboost
or scikit-learn

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:43.770
on Cloud ML Engine.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.610
And again, it basically serves
as on-demand infrastructure

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:48.780
for machine learning.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:53.340
So you can set up clusters
to do your training jobs.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.530
Once you've trained
your model, then you

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:59.130
can take that same model and
deploy it as a cloud end point,

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:00.990
as a rest end point.

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:04.110
So you can seamlessly transition
from training to prediction.

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:07.279
It offers some built-in
model version management.

00:11:07.279 --> 00:11:09.570
And then basically, you're
still just using TensorFlow,

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:10.950
so you could take that model.

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:12.070
There's nothing locking you in.

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:14.250
You can take that model
and download it and run it

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:18.450
elsewhere on premise, you
know, even on another cloud.

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:23.640
So for our purposes
here, for scalability,

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.730
the pros of ML Engine
are that it's very easy

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:30.180
to scale up in terms of asking
for more capacity for more

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:30.700
workers.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:34.380
You just basically specify
that in your training job.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:37.230
And it's also nice that
the serving capability

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:37.910
is automatic.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:39.750
There's been some
recent improvements

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:44.820
in things like startup latency
and other aspects of serving.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.620
We're just about to announce
increased capability

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:53.460
to use GPUs for Cloud ML Engine.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:56.520
So it's got a number
of advantages.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.320
The cons, I would say,
are associated with,

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.694
you know, using distributed
TensorFlow, which is just not

00:12:00.694 --> 00:12:02.190
easy, no matter
how you slice it.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.981
Again, we're working
on the TensorFlow team

00:12:03.981 --> 00:12:06.060
to make that easier,
but it's still

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:07.750
got some gotchas
associated with it.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.750
We're going to talk a bit
more about that later,

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:12.360
and how you can navigate that.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:13.980
There are some
limited limitations,

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.140
you know, like limited
machine types, which again,

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:17.400
we're working on resolving.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.790
So again, there's
some limitations

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:25.830
in the service that make it
not ideal for some cases.

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:28.770
But it's still quite a
very powerful service.

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:31.650
You know, with serving
for example, again,

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.720
ML Engine, in terms
of scalability,

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.702
you're not in charge
of provisioning

00:12:36.702 --> 00:12:37.660
the number of machines.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.470
There are some knobs and
parameters that you can tweak,

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:42.840
but essentially it
scales according

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:46.400
to your load, which is great.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.529
All right, for the
section on Kubernetes

00:12:48.529 --> 00:12:50.320
for machine learning,
I'm going to bring up

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.860
my colleague Drew [? Houghton ?]
on the CEML specialist team,

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:55.485
and have him tell
you a bit about that.

00:12:55.485 --> 00:12:56.693
[? DREW HOUGHTON: ?] Awesome.

00:12:56.693 --> 00:12:57.920
Thank you, Lukman.

00:12:57.920 --> 00:12:59.680
So for this section,
we're going to talk

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:03.320
about the best of two
worlds that I work

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:04.570
with customers quite a lot on.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:06.940
One is Kubernetes, and
one is machine learning.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:08.860
And let's take a look
at how Kubernetes

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:10.570
can help solve some
of the problems,

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:14.930
totally in open source and
across multiple clouds.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:16.970
So ML in production,
for those of us

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:20.280
that have tried or have
done ML in production,

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.090
we know that we care about these
three things-- composability,

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:25.690
portability, and scalability.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:27.830
And let's talk
about each of those.

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.350
First is composability.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:34.520
When you solve an ML
problem on your laptop,

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.305
it's easy to solve
some problems maybe

00:13:36.305 --> 00:13:40.880
on Kaggle on your laptop, small
to medium dataset, with what I

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.472
call, it's kind of what I
call toy machine learning.

00:13:43.472 --> 00:13:45.680
You solve the problem, maybe
you won the competition,

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:49.160
but that's not the same
as taking your solution,

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.600
scaling it, and
making it worthwhile,

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.170
and frankly, you know, worth
the money and the effort

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:55.110
for the enterprise.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.494
There's a lot of things that
come after that, not just

00:13:57.494 --> 00:13:58.910
the tuning and
building the model,

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:01.910
but deploying it at scale,
refreshing your model

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:06.070
every week, day, 15 minutes, if
you have cold start problems.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:10.520
AB testing and model evaluation,
and serving at scale, right?

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:13.430
The reality is that to do each
of these different portions

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:15.890
of the pipeline, there's
different systems.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:16.950
We have data ingestion.

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:18.884
So you know, where's
the data coming from?

00:14:18.884 --> 00:14:19.550
Is it real time?

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:20.300
Is it streaming?

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:22.400
Is it from a variety
of different systems?

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.220
Who's putting it all
together in a data lake?

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:28.260
We have our data
analysis and validation.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:30.920
So this is exploring
the data, understanding

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:34.190
what we can actually model
or glean from the data.

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.440
Then we actually have the
model building itself,

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.106
the training, the
hyperparameter tuning,

00:14:38.106 --> 00:14:40.070
and lastly the serving,
serving at scale,

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:42.230
keeping the model fresh.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:45.680
Now in practice, these are
often different people, if not

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:49.930
different entire teams, doing
this at scale in production,

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:50.430
right?

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.388
So there's different very
different skill sets,

00:14:52.388 --> 00:14:53.880
very different systems.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:55.160
And it makes sense, right?

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:57.120
These are very
different problems.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:01.570
One of the promises of ML
is that we sort of unify

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:02.400
a lot of our data.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.590
We apply an algorithm
to a business problem.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:07.510
We do it in real time.

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:09.070
Now, this is great.

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.370
But when you have lots
of teams involved,

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:14.779
lots of systems involved, it can
become more difficult to scale.

00:15:14.779 --> 00:15:16.320
Every time you deploy
a model, do you

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.120
have to get every team involved?

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:19.950
So we care about composability.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:22.470
Essentially, reusing
different pieces

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:26.730
from each stage with sort of
limited engagement or friction

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.170
each time I want
to build a model.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.600
For example,
building a model that

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:32.190
can be used by a
software engineer

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.950
in the future on similar data
without having to go back

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.950
to that ML researcher.

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.754
Now, some of us also care
a lot about portability.

00:15:40.754 --> 00:15:42.670
So just show of hands
in the room, who's using

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:46.240
Kubernetes or has
explored Kubernetes?

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:47.180
A lot of hands, right.

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:48.630
That's awesome to see.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:50.630
And these days,
definitely the best

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:52.770
place to run
containers at scale.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:54.710
One of the great
things about Kubernetes

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:59.242
is the way we've
abstracted the compute,

00:15:59.242 --> 00:16:00.950
you can take your
application and move it

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:05.900
very easily from your laptop
to the on-premise cluster

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.252
to one of various
cloud providers.

00:16:08.252 --> 00:16:10.460
And this is something very
important to lots and lots

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:11.210
of organizations.

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:14.130
We hear this again and again.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:16.710
Also, we have great
primitives in Kubernetes

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:18.495
with dealing with
things like this.

00:16:18.495 --> 00:16:22.003
So Joe Beata, one of the
co-founders of Kubernetes,

00:16:22.003 --> 00:16:24.502
says, the way I think about it,
every difference between dev

00:16:24.502 --> 00:16:28.500
stage and production will
eventually result in an outage.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:30.960
And it's very easy in
Kubernetes, at least

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.630
easier than in a lot
of other systems,

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.570
of treating things like
dev staging and prod

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:38.940
as sort of first-class citizens
that the app understands,

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:44.040
and helps to limit the
problems you're going to face.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.910
So let's talk about portability
with machine learning.

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:51.660
Now, because machine
learning is such a compute-

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:54.390
and performance-intensive
application,

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:57.180
we actually care a lot about
all the different pieces

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:01.030
of our system, all the
way down to the hardware.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.780
But it's not just the hardware,
it's up to the drivers.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:05.940
Anyone had problems
installing drivers,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.890
and takes a long time
before you can actually

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:09.240
start working on your problem?

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.040
Can be quite a nightmare.

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:14.079
The frameworks, they
change very quickly.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:17.585
You know, this is a very
rapidly moving space.

00:17:17.585 --> 00:17:18.960
There's a lot of
effort that goes

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:22.060
into building the stack on a
laptop, getting data training,

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:23.640
getting the model working.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.040
You're super lucky if
it converges right away.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:30.370
But again, this is great for
solving a toy ML problem.

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.280
What happens if I want
a skill, or I want

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:35.090
to deploy this in production?

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:37.540
Now, I'm dealing with
different environments, right?

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:39.700
Maybe I have a training
rig in the back,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:40.930
you know, cooling the office.

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:43.300
Maybe I'm using an
on prem cluster.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:47.380
We have existing significant
GPU or CPU investments.

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:49.657
Maybe this isn't
a cloud provider.

00:17:49.657 --> 00:17:52.240
We're multiplying the complexity
of keeping these environments

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:53.550
in sync.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.900
It was already hard enough
to get the laptop up

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:57.775
and running that
environment perfect for what

00:17:57.775 --> 00:17:59.370
we wanted to do.

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:00.745
It gets even more complex.

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:05.740
So pop quiz.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:07.830
What's really good at
composability, portability,

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:10.030
and scalability?

00:18:10.030 --> 00:18:11.780
Kubernetes, you got it.

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:13.860
Containers in Kubernetes.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.070
And guess what?

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:19.340
Google runs all of our machine
learning on containers,

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:23.040
and essentially, you know,
Borg, but the influence

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:24.540
behind Kubernetes.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.190
So this can be done.

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:27.820
And we have some great news.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:31.040
Kubernetes today has a
lot of great primitives

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:34.310
and support for machine
learning workloads.

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:37.310
We support accelerators
in a very easy to use,

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:38.960
extensible manner.

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.600
So GPUs are in beta,
TPUs are in alpha.

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:46.340
With just one line in
your communities manifest,

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:50.120
you can request
GPUs for your pods.

00:18:50.120 --> 00:18:52.970
It's really easy to
schedule lots of GPUs

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:56.010
and bring lots of horsepower
to your workloads.

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.290
The existing controllers
in Kubernetes

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:01.660
also simplify a lot of the
DevOps challenges we face.

00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:03.240
Let's remember,
the reason we want

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.250
to use Kubernetes here
is we get lots of wins

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.020
from the infrastructure
standpoint,

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:08.520
from the repeatability
standpoint,

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.240
and from the DevOps standpoint.

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:14.130
We want to apply those wins to
our machine learning workloads.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:16.810
Some of the ways it does
this is Kubernetes Jobs.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:18.630
So when I create
a Kubernetes Job,

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.420
maybe this is a training
job, Kubernetes will then

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:26.500
go create a pod or multiple pods
to try and complete that job.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.620
If a node fails, let's say
I'm using preemptable nodes

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:32.860
or preemptable GPUs,
it will take that job,

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:34.780
it'll schedule it
somewhere else for me.

00:19:34.780 --> 00:19:36.790
If I'm using, say, the
TensorFlow Estimator

00:19:36.790 --> 00:19:39.790
API, I'm checkpointing
regularly,

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:42.469
my job is all of a sudden
stateless, in a way.

00:19:42.469 --> 00:19:44.260
You know, Kubernetes
will recreate the pod,

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:47.580
it'll keep training somewhere
else in the data center.

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.330
We also have
Kubernetes deployments,

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.060
which are great for serving.

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.670
So you tell Kubernetes what
your deployment looks like,

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:56.310
where the container is
or where the model is,

00:19:56.310 --> 00:19:58.560
how fast you want
to scale it, and it

00:19:58.560 --> 00:19:59.850
does all the rest for you.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.560
As your load
increases, Kubernetes

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:05.060
will create more
pods on your behalf.

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:06.020
It also scales.

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:08.160
At this point, I
think Kubernetes

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:10.190
will scale up to 5,000
nodes, and that's

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:12.940
increasing quite rapidly.

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:16.280
And we also have container-based
images for ML workloads.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:18.214
So in some cases,
you don't actually

00:20:18.214 --> 00:20:19.630
have to build your
own containers.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:22.960
You can use an off-the-shelf
TensorFlow CPU container,

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:24.730
and then there's
a GPU container.

00:20:24.730 --> 00:20:28.090
And if you're comfortable
with code control,

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:30.249
you can actually just load
your code up at runtime.

00:20:30.249 --> 00:20:32.290
There's benefits to building
your own containers,

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:34.510
but you don't even
need to to get started

00:20:34.510 --> 00:20:38.420
using Kubernetes for ML.

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:39.340
So this is great.

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:40.970
We have the right primitives.

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:44.894
We have the right tools
that Kubernetes supports.

00:20:44.894 --> 00:20:46.560
But a lot of things
I just talked about,

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.230
if you're a data scientist or
machine learning researcher,

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:52.870
might not be as
familiar to you, right?

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:55.210
Maybe you feel like I need
to learn about containers,

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:57.510
container building,
end points for serving,

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:00.250
persistent volumes, scaling,
all these things, before I

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:04.684
can be really effective with
machine learning on Kubernetes.

00:21:04.684 --> 00:21:06.100
You know, we see
the promise here.

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:09.160
We like that we can get
a consistent environment

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:11.372
or we can get a similar
environment on prem

00:21:11.372 --> 00:21:13.330
to the cloud, but there's
a lot of other things

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:15.490
we still have to build.

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:18.400
So the good news is,
we have an answer.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.180
And that is Kubeflow.

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:24.180
So Kubeflow is an
open source project

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:26.640
whose goal is to make
it easy for everyone

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:30.180
to learn, deploy, and manage
portable, distributed machine

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.580
learning on Kubernetes.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:34.920
So basically, to get all those
infrastructure Kubernetes

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:37.920
wins, but make it
really easy for those

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:39.904
that aren't Kubernetes experts.

00:21:39.904 --> 00:21:42.570
And this is a trend we're seeing
more and more in the Kubernetes

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:45.120
world, higher-level
frameworks that

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.520
make it really easy to
solve a particular problem.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:51.950
So what does Kubeflow get us?

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:53.270
It gets us composability.

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:58.040
We have a variety of existing
popular open source tools.

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:00.050
I'll dive into those
in a little bit.

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:03.950
We use ksonnet for packaging
and easily setting up

00:22:03.950 --> 00:22:05.760
your environment.

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:09.410
So all of a sudden, des,
staging, production, laptop,

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.510
are all first-class citizens.

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:13.040
And with just a
couple of commands,

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:17.380
you can deploy to each of
them the same exact code.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:18.600
It's very portable.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:21.790
Kubeflow is totally open source,
using totally open source

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:22.960
components.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:24.340
And a lot of those
components are

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:26.780
cloud native, Kubernetes
native components.

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:29.320
I'll talk about those
in just a second.

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:33.010
What's great is that the
huge community that's

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:37.330
built up around Kubernetes
is solving your deployment

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:40.150
problems so you can focus on
the actual machine learning

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:41.770
problem.

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:43.490
And lastly, it's
scalable, right?

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:48.090
TensorFlow already supports
CPU, GPU, TPU, distributed.

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.110
Kubernetes scales like crazy.

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:53.820
We can use that power to
handle our serving workloads

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:56.020
and our training workloads.

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:58.450
So what does this look
like, and how does

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:01.316
this solve this whole mess?

00:23:01.316 --> 00:23:02.690
Well, for those
of you that know,

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:04.820
Kubernetes will actually
solve a lot of it for you.

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:07.375
That's why we were looking
at Kubernetes as a solution,

00:23:07.375 --> 00:23:08.030
right?

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:09.446
So I'll kind of
go back and forth.

00:23:09.446 --> 00:23:11.570
Kubernetes will give you
a pretty consistent API

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:13.377
up to the runtime.

00:23:13.377 --> 00:23:15.210
So I don't have to worry
about this anymore.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:21.350
I have abstracted my compute
to a really nice level,

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:23.930
and I don't have to worry
about keeping it in sync.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:27.590
Now, that said, you're still
having to worry traditionally

00:23:27.590 --> 00:23:32.860
about the framework, what my
storage is, maybe drivers.

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:34.910
Kubeflow, with just
a couple of commands,

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:37.850
can be installed on
Minikube on your laptop.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:40.850
Minikube is a local mini
Kubernetes cluster running

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:44.570
in a VM, but you get the
same API on your laptop

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:47.990
as if you were in a 5,000 node
cluster with thousands of GPUs.

00:23:47.990 --> 00:23:49.520
A couple commands,
you can install

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:52.760
Kubeflow on your laptop, on your
on-premise existing cluster,

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.830
on various cloud clusters.

00:23:54.830 --> 00:23:58.610
And now you have a consistent
ML-ready environment

00:23:58.610 --> 00:24:00.770
across wherever you do compute.

00:24:03.379 --> 00:24:04.670
So what's included in Kubeflow?

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:08.630
So we actually just
released version 0.2.

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:10.600
So we are earlier in
the days of Kubeflow,

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:13.010
but this is developing
very rapidly,

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:15.770
because customers do want a
solution that spans across

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:16.760
clouds.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:18.680
For some customers,
being totally open source

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:20.180
is very important.

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:23.150
Running in multiple clouds
and on prem is very important.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:25.580
Same reasons we've seen the
popularity with Kubernetes

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:26.480
in general.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:28.220
What's included
today is JupyterHub

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:30.680
for collaborative,
interactive training.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:33.145
A TensorFlow training
controller, so now

00:24:33.145 --> 00:24:34.520
you don't have to
tell Kubernetes

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:36.350
what a TensorFlow
training job looks like

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:38.510
or how to do
distributed TensorFlow.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:42.350
You just tell it, hey, here's
my code, run it distributed.

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:44.870
Very similar to how you would
do with Cloud ML Engine.

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:46.100
Same thing with serving.

00:24:46.100 --> 00:24:47.660
You don't have to
tell Kubernetes

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:49.160
what TensorFlow serving is.

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:53.370
You just say, here's my model,
go serve it and auto-scale it.

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:55.130
We have Argo for workflows.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.560
So creating a
consistent pipeline

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:59.960
from pre-processing to
training, hyperparameter

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:01.510
tuning and validation.

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:05.030
SeldonCore for complex
inference and non TF-models.

00:25:05.030 --> 00:25:08.180
I want to stress that this
is not just for TensorFlow.

00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:09.950
We have PyTorch in alpha.

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:12.590
We definitely have people
running scikit, xgboost,

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:13.460
and SeldonCore.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:16.670
You can run whatever
frameworks you want.

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:21.410
Also in 0.2, we released
hyperparamter tuning

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:23.580
and Ambassador for
reverse proxies.

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:27.212
So this is a very
rapidly moving project.

00:25:27.212 --> 00:25:28.920
All right, and then
for our next section,

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:34.427
I'm going to invite Kevin
up for scaling training.

00:25:34.427 --> 00:25:37.010
LUKMAN RAMSEY: So I'm going to
have Kevin come up now and talk

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:38.840
about scaling
training, in particular

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:42.680
about the challenges of doing
distributed training on GPUs.

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:44.815
Thanks, Kevin.

00:25:44.815 --> 00:25:46.450
[? KEVIN TSAI: ?]
Thanks, Lukman.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.130
OK.

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:52.970
One of the very common questions
that we get from the community

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:58.670
is I had GPU, and I just
upgraded my VM24 GPUs,

00:25:58.670 --> 00:26:00.300
but training is just as slow.

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:02.360
What's going on, right?

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:04.670
If you take a step
back and try to think

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:06.650
about what you're
doing with TensorFlow,

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:09.700
you're creating a graph and
then you're executing the graph,

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:10.610
right?

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:12.800
And by default if
you don't specify

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:15.250
where your graph
goes and TensorFlow

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:19.160
sees one or more GPUs, it will
throw the operations in GPU 0,

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:19.970
right?

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:23.510
So there's a really nice tool
from NVIDIA called NVIDIA SMI.

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:25.540
If you run that during
training, you'll

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:27.950
be able to see how
busy your GPUs are.

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:29.990
If you run it on
multiple GPUs and you

00:26:29.990 --> 00:26:32.480
see something like this,
right, that probably

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:38.170
means that you created a graph
that only had busyness in GPU 0

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:38.810
right?

00:26:38.810 --> 00:26:41.190
So that's actually
the expected behavior

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:44.030
if you don't specify
parallelization

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:45.355
over multiple GPUs.

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:52.030
So let's take a look at a
very simple model, right?

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:54.190
And we use this to
describe what we're trying

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:56.830
to do in parallelization.

00:26:56.830 --> 00:27:00.850
One of the ways to parallelize
across multiple GPS

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:02.890
is called data parallelism.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:04.450
And basically what
you're doing is

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:09.580
you are replicating your entire
model across two or more GPUs,

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:12.800
and then you go ahead and
divide up the data coming in

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:14.240
into smaller batches.

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:17.750
So let's say, for example,
if your batch size is 256,

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:20.990
and you split it over GPUs,
you have 128 go to the GPU 0,

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:23.470
128 go to GPU 1, right?

00:27:23.470 --> 00:27:26.630
So that's pretty
straightforward.

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:29.350
The other way of parallelizing
is called model parallelism,

00:27:29.350 --> 00:27:33.790
and typically, you wouldn't use
this unless you get to a point

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:36.160
where your model is
really big, or you're

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:38.897
running out of memory and
GPUs and so forth, right?

00:27:38.897 --> 00:27:41.230
And it's actually also possible
to put model parallelism

00:27:41.230 --> 00:27:43.780
and data parallelism together.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:47.860
Now, one of the challenges
when where we're splitting data

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:51.550
into two or more GPUs,
right, is at the end,

00:27:51.550 --> 00:27:54.767
you're going to end up
with multiple result sets.

00:27:54.767 --> 00:27:56.350
So if you think about
the predictions,

00:27:56.350 --> 00:27:59.200
you started with 256,
128 goes to one side

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:00.939
and 128 goes to the other side.

00:28:00.939 --> 00:28:02.980
The prediction's actually
pretty straightforward.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:05.200
You just concatenate
that, right?

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:08.720
But gradient's a little
bit more complex.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:10.510
So what we normally
do with gradients

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.210
coming from the two GPUs, we'll
go ahead and average that,

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:15.250
right?

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:17.590
And one of the things that
we also found at Google

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:24.670
is, to have the best
chance of convergence,

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:28.180
what we want to do is we want
to do synchronous replication,

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:32.080
meaning that after each
batch, you want to calculate

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:33.760
the gradient,
apply the gradient,

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:37.510
update the parameters, and then
go to the next batch, right?

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:39.290
That's the best performance.

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:41.800
But that also means that
the gradient averaging

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:43.240
becomes a blocking process.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.450
So it's very important that
we have a very fast method

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:50.000
of applying the gradients.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:52.730
So one of the ways that
we could do that is just

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:58.460
go ahead and use a CPU as
the consolidation mechanism.

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:03.680
So after each batch, the GPUs
would each copy the gradients

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:07.100
to the CPU, and the CPU would
go ahead and average it out,

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:12.380
make the update stop, and
redistribute the parameters

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:14.220
back across all the GPUs.

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:15.710
And that's one way to do it.

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:17.790
But as you already
kind of see over here,

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:20.180
if we increase the
number of GPUs,

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:22.220
you're going to increase
the number of copies

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:24.170
you have to average out,
and then that becomes

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.540
a bottleneck, right?

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:28.970
So a more performant
way of doing it

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:33.050
is to use an all reduce type
of mechanism to go ahead

00:29:33.050 --> 00:29:35.540
and do the distribution, right?

00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:39.140
The other advantage of doing
an all reduce type mechanism

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:42.542
is that you could take advantage
of the very fast NV link,

00:29:42.542 --> 00:29:44.000
right, whereas like
if you're going

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:46.380
to let the CPU do
the consolidation,

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:48.020
you have to go through PCIE.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:52.400
So when it comes to machine
learning, PCIE is slow, right?

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:53.210
Just remember that.

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:54.731
PCIE is actually really slow.

00:29:54.731 --> 00:29:55.230
OK.

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.970
So if you take a step back, here
are the kind of the options.

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:08.430
If you were to go ahead and
choose a distribution strategy

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:11.992
or distribution
method today, here

00:30:11.992 --> 00:30:13.950
are kind of like, at the
high level, what would

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:15.060
you could choose from.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:18.420
So if you have a single
server, it's not too hard,

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:21.400
a lot of built-in capabilities
within TensorFlow and Keras

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:22.470
that's available.

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:27.060
So ever since 1.5, we
have replicate_model_fn.

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:30.690
And within Keras, Keras actually
had their multi-GPU model

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:34.045
strategy even before
TensorFlow did.

00:30:34.045 --> 00:30:35.670
But in both cases,
you have to remember

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:40.980
that these are
CPU-bound, right, meaning

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:45.790
they use a CPU as a
consolidation strategy,

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:49.680
whereas in TensorFlow 1.8, we
introduced mirrored strategy,

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:53.400
which is part of a larger family
called distribution strategy.

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:56.490
And mirrored strategy
currently is single server,

00:30:56.490 --> 00:31:00.210
but multiple GPUs, and it
uses the all reduced type

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:03.250
of mechanism as well.

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:05.530
Now, if one server
is not good enough,

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.940
there are also options for
multiple servers, right?

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:13.170
And so you might have heard
[INAUDIBLE] talk about TPUs.

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:17.700
So TPU is one of the options to
go ahead and go very parallel.

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:21.180
The other option, there's
an open source project

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:23.310
called Horovod put together
by a couple of really

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:25.080
smart engineers at Uber.

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:28.200
So that gives you the ability
to scale over multiple servers

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:30.450
with multiple GPUs as well.

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:32.650
And distribution
[INAUDIBLE],, again,

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:38.290
is going to be offered as a
multi-server option as well.

00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:41.640
Now, one mention is, if you
look at the older TensorFlow

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:45.120
documentation, there's
mention of parameter server

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:48.270
and workers.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:55.080
Because this method is
not as effective in coming

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:57.900
to convergence compared
to the other methods, what

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:00.561
we recommend is, that's
probably not the first solution

00:32:00.561 --> 00:32:01.560
that you should look at.

00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:09.109
OK, so just want to
spend a little time

00:32:09.109 --> 00:32:10.150
on distribution strategy.

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:14.280
So what we realized
is a lot of users

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:16.530
already have a lot of
code that's written,

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:18.630
and we want to be
able to make it

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:22.239
so that you can kind of shoehorn
this capability into your code

00:32:22.239 --> 00:32:23.280
without a lot of changes.

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:28.950
So what we've done is, we
have put this capability

00:32:28.950 --> 00:32:30.120
into run_config.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:33.510
So you specify a distribution
strategy-- in this case,

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:34.980
mirrored strategy--

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:37.770
and you would go ahead and put
that as one of the parameters

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:40.880
within run_config, and you
create the estimator that way,

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:41.790
right?

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:43.830
And if you're using
Keras, we also

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:46.625
support the model to
estimator conversion,

00:32:46.625 --> 00:32:48.000
and within there,
you'll go ahead

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:49.560
and specify run_config as well.

00:32:53.344 --> 00:32:53.844
Thanks.

00:32:57.190 --> 00:33:00.020
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Thank you, Kevin.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:01.820
Thank you.

00:33:01.820 --> 00:33:05.760
So we heard a lot of really
interesting information

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:09.300
about how to scale
using Kubernetes

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:12.192
as a common architecture
and Kubeflow.

00:33:12.192 --> 00:33:14.400
And we also heard some really
interesting information

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:19.140
about distributing
over multiple GPUs.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:23.580
And again, we
will, as a company,

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:26.280
always continue to
support and encourage

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:30.630
people to leverage GPUs on
our Google Cloud environment.

00:33:30.630 --> 00:33:34.380
We also, as you know, provide
our own hardware acceleration

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:36.810
option TPUs, and so I'm
going talk just a little

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:38.820
bit about that as well.

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:44.880
So TPUs are now at
version 1 and 2.

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:48.570
Both of those have been
deployed in Google Cloud.

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:51.450
Well, TPU version 1 was
actually deployed internally

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:53.850
in Google's
infrastructure onboard.

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:58.200
TPU version 2 has been made
available to our customers

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:00.570
at Google Cloud, and today,
we announced TPU version 3.

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:02.361
Actually, I think we
announced that at I/O.

00:34:02.361 --> 00:34:06.810
But anyway, we're continuing
on developing and improving

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:08.699
that architecture,
and you should

00:34:08.699 --> 00:34:11.790
expect TPU 3.0 to
be launched sometime

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.976
later this year, in cloud.

00:34:14.976 --> 00:34:17.100
And the way we make this
available to our customers

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:19.710
on cloud is something called
Cloud TPU, which is essentially

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:23.969
a service that wraps around TPU
resources that live in a pod

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:31.830
and make it easy for you to do
your training jobs using TPUs.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:34.350
This is a quick
example of sort of what

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:36.210
that looks like in code.

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:38.080
Essentially, as Kevin
mentioned, you know,

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:42.929
it's just simple as basically
using a TPU estimator instead

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:45.060
of a regular estimator.

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:46.860
And then there are
some other things

00:34:46.860 --> 00:34:49.860
you have to do to
connect to a cloud TPU,

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:53.520
but it's designed to be
relatively simple to do.

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.800
And we do have a lot
of customers now taking

00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:56.969
advantage of TPUs.

00:34:56.969 --> 00:34:59.620
So for certain
kinds of workloads,

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:01.200
It can be quite effective.

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.830
Again, this is an example
of another code change

00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:06.600
that one could use in order
to take advantage of TPUs.

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:11.330
So just using a TPU
version of the optimizer.

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:14.590
Again, it's not for
every problem, right?

00:35:14.590 --> 00:35:19.130
You need to have enough data
and enough need for scale

00:35:19.130 --> 00:35:22.670
in order to justify the
work that you do in order

00:35:22.670 --> 00:35:25.730
to customize for TPU.

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:28.880
So again, situations where
you've got very large models

00:35:28.880 --> 00:35:32.540
with very large batch
sizes, the models

00:35:32.540 --> 00:35:37.040
need to be able to run on TPUs,
so not all models currently do.

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:39.240
Just worth pointing out.

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:42.270
We will get there eventually,
but even as of today,

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:44.820
there are certain operations
that would not be supported.

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:49.070
So you may have a model
that just won't run on TPU.

00:35:49.070 --> 00:35:55.190
And you don't want to use Cloud
TPU for very small models,

00:35:55.190 --> 00:35:59.180
or models with lots of branches,
or again, that use operations

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:00.610
that aren't supported.

00:36:00.610 --> 00:36:01.110
All right.

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:03.860
We can talk a little bit about
input pipeline performance.

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:07.250
That's another key
aspect of scaling

00:36:07.250 --> 00:36:08.780
with respect to training.

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:10.370
So the first point
I want to make

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:14.880
is that if you're not using
the TF data API, you should be,

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:15.380
all right?

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:19.190
This is, as of about a
year ago or so, basically

00:36:19.190 --> 00:36:23.290
our recommended architecture
for building data pipelines

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:25.070
in TensorFlow.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:28.430
Again, there are older
methods out there

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:30.480
using input cues and so on.

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:33.320
But we really strongly
recommend that you use this API

00:36:33.320 --> 00:36:36.180
to specify your data pipelines.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:38.780
But just using
this API by itself

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:41.690
doesn't necessarily
get you there, right?

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:47.690
So this is an example of a
ResNet 50 data set API, coded,

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:48.470
you know, simply.

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:50.844
I mean, there's nothing wrong
with the way this is coded,

00:36:50.844 --> 00:36:54.630
but you get through this
about 150 images per second.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:37:00.230
And really, you should be able
to expect for this image net

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:03.150
problem, you know, something
over 1,000 images per second.

00:37:03.150 --> 00:37:05.370
So what is the issue
with this code,

00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:08.210
and what can we
do to optimize it?

00:37:08.210 --> 00:37:10.520
Well, again, the
way, when you're

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:12.890
approaching the optimization
of input pipelines,

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:16.460
we suggest that you
think is like ETL.

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:20.300
Basically, separate the
process of extracting the data

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:23.140
from the storage, transforming
that data to prepare it

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:25.160
for training, which
typically has to happen,

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:27.230
and then loading that
data into the accelerator.

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:29.090
Think of those in
distinct phases,

00:37:29.090 --> 00:37:30.980
because different parts
of the infrastructure

00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:32.630
are going to be
dedicated to that.

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:35.376
And so if you can segment
those processes such

00:37:35.376 --> 00:37:37.250
that those different
pieces of infrastructure

00:37:37.250 --> 00:37:39.131
are allowed to
operate in parallel,

00:37:39.131 --> 00:37:41.130
then you're going to get
much better throughput.

00:37:41.130 --> 00:37:46.460
So here's an example where
the operations are serialized

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:49.710
at a very high level, you know,
with the time going forward.

00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:53.330
And the extract transform and
load, and then train steps,

00:37:53.330 --> 00:37:56.180
are all synchronous
with each other.

00:37:56.180 --> 00:37:58.460
Here-- and of
course, you would see

00:37:58.460 --> 00:38:00.170
that the various
components of the system

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:02.570
are going to be idle at any
given point in that cycle,

00:38:02.570 --> 00:38:03.070
right?

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:04.040
Your CPU is idle.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:06.500
If your CPU is doing
the data transformation,

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:09.500
it's idle while it's waiting
for the next data set

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:10.680
to come through.

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:13.690
And similarly, if you're
using hardware acceleration

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:15.980
in this kind of
scheme, you're going

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:19.280
to see low GPU
utilization, right?

00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:21.870
That's when you run
that NVIDIA SMI tool,

00:38:21.870 --> 00:38:25.780
and you see something like
25% or 40% utilization.

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:29.250
It's often because of
input pipelining issues.

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:31.970
So this is what you want
to see instead, where

00:38:31.970 --> 00:38:36.290
the processes of extraction,
transformation, and loading

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:39.860
are all parallelized
sufficiently such

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:43.700
that your training
infrastructure, your GPUs,

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:47.970
or your custom hardware, the
utilization is maximized.

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:49.790
So again, we don't
have time here

00:38:49.790 --> 00:38:53.660
to talk about all of
the tricks of the trade

00:38:53.660 --> 00:38:57.470
that you use to do this,
but just let's note here

00:38:57.470 --> 00:39:00.110
that the dataset API
is really designed

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:03.570
to parallelize in the same
way that we just discussed.

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:07.370
So again, if you parallelize
that mapping operation that's

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:09.770
expressed here across
multiple cores,

00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:15.020
just by adding a parameter
to the function call,

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:17.240
that's enough to achieve
parallelization there,

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:18.740
similarly.

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:23.620
And that by itself gets you from
150 images per second to 600,

00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:24.200
right.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:25.366
But there's more you can do.

00:39:25.366 --> 00:39:29.570
So you can also parallelize
the reading, essentially

00:39:29.570 --> 00:39:31.340
the extract step.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:35.330
And you can parallelize the
prefetch step that basically

00:39:35.330 --> 00:39:39.590
allows the data set to be queued
up so you can feed it faster

00:39:39.590 --> 00:39:41.360
into the GPU.

00:39:41.360 --> 00:39:44.134
And with those two changes,
just with the same code,

00:39:44.134 --> 00:39:45.800
again, it's basically
the same pipeline,

00:39:45.800 --> 00:39:49.520
just with some extra
parallelization primitives

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:51.800
or parameters added to it.

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:55.250
We get to over 1,750
images per second.

00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:57.680
So just the point
to make here is

00:39:57.680 --> 00:40:00.314
that it's very critical to
tune your input pipelines,

00:40:00.314 --> 00:40:02.480
and that can make a huge,
huge difference in the way

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:05.390
that your training
process scales.

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:09.400
So we're not going to talk
about scaling prediction.

00:40:09.400 --> 00:40:14.020
And this is a number of
interesting topics here.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:17.920
We're going to talk about
how you optimize your model.

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:19.780
There's a tool within
TensorFlow that's

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:22.570
been around for a while that's
not probably widely appreciated

00:40:22.570 --> 00:40:25.030
called Graph Transform Tool
that we would encourage

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:28.792
you to make use of to optimize
your models for prediction.

00:40:28.792 --> 00:40:30.625
We cold talk a little
bit about quantization

00:40:30.625 --> 00:40:33.310
and what that is, why
you care about that,

00:40:33.310 --> 00:40:35.640
what you'd use that for.

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:38.590
TensorFlow Lite, which is a new
framework that's specifically

00:40:38.590 --> 00:40:40.810
targeted to make models
run fast on mobile.

00:40:40.810 --> 00:40:43.330
Actually, it's a
framework for quantization

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:45.202
on mobile specifically.

00:40:45.202 --> 00:40:46.660
We're going to talk
about TensorRT,

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:50.440
which is a great framework
from our partners at NVIDIA

00:40:50.440 --> 00:40:54.550
that makes it really fast to
serve models that leverage some

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:57.857
of the capabilities of
the NVIDIA hardware.

00:40:57.857 --> 00:40:59.440
And then finally,
we're going to touch

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:01.440
on some serving
optimizations that you

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:04.570
can make the TF Serving to
make serving process go faster.

00:41:04.570 --> 00:41:06.370
So what is Graph Transform Tool?

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:08.770
Well, it's actually present
in the TensorFlow code base.

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:12.675
It has been since
I think pre-1.0.

00:41:12.675 --> 00:41:14.050
And it has a number
of operations

00:41:14.050 --> 00:41:16.840
that are very useful
when you're optimizing

00:41:16.840 --> 00:41:19.090
your graph for serving.

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:20.800
So one of the
things that you want

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:23.070
to do, typically
the serving graph,

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:26.020
there's some components
in the training graph

00:41:26.020 --> 00:41:28.600
that aren't necessary
for the serving graph.

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:34.000
And GTT tool allows you
to strip those unneeded

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:36.350
nodes out of your graph, just
to make it more efficient,

00:41:36.350 --> 00:41:40.310
make it a lighter footprint
and faster for serving.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:42.980
Again, it will do
graph optimization,

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:46.840
so it will remove nodes
that aren't doing anything,

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:48.050
the identity nodes.

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:49.510
There's various other
nodes that are typically

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:52.093
included in the graph that are
needed for training that aren't

00:41:52.093 --> 00:41:55.750
needed for predict if you use
batch normalization, which

00:41:55.750 --> 00:41:59.680
is another topic that I'd love
to have spent some time on.

00:41:59.680 --> 00:42:01.750
If you're not using
batch normalization

00:42:01.750 --> 00:42:04.090
and you're training a
deep neural network,

00:42:04.090 --> 00:42:05.346
you're not doing it right.

00:42:05.346 --> 00:42:06.970
You're not following
the best practice.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:08.469
You need to use
batch normalization.

00:42:08.469 --> 00:42:10.410
Let's just leave it at that.

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:12.372
There is some
information out there

00:42:12.372 --> 00:42:14.080
that you can go find
to incorporate that.

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:16.390
It's pretty easy to
do in TensorFlow.

00:42:16.390 --> 00:42:20.530
You will find anywhere from
20% to sometimes more percent

00:42:20.530 --> 00:42:23.710
uplift in both training accuracy
and speed to convergence, just

00:42:23.710 --> 00:42:25.220
by using batch normalization.

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:26.140
It's a best practice.

00:42:26.140 --> 00:42:31.310
There's no researcher in deep
learning that's not using it.

00:42:31.310 --> 00:42:35.500
So it does some
calculation that makes

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:37.310
batch normalization go faster.

00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:40.240
So that's another capability
of the Graph Transform Tool,

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:41.860
and several other
useful transforms,

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:44.740
including the ability
to quantize your graph.

00:42:44.740 --> 00:42:46.840
So what is quantization?

00:42:46.840 --> 00:42:54.720
Well, it turns out that
weights, 32-bit weights,

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:59.250
are quite useful for
training, but for serving,

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:02.520
we can often get away
with much lower precision,

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:04.290
like even down to 8-bit weights.

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:08.190
Actually, there are folks out
there doing 1-bit weights,

00:43:08.190 --> 00:43:09.430
believe it or not.

00:43:09.430 --> 00:43:13.350
But certainly without
losing a lot of accuracy,

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:18.030
but achieving a huge speed
up in serving performance,

00:43:18.030 --> 00:43:20.030
you can go from 32-bit
weights to 8-bit weights.

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:22.321
This is a very common practice,
particularly again when

00:43:22.321 --> 00:43:25.020
you're deploying on smaller
devices like mobile devices

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:27.180
or devices on the edge,
it's even more important,

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:29.530
because model size
really matters.

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:32.849
But so you get a big uplift
in both speed and [? not, ?]

00:43:32.849 --> 00:43:34.890
sometimes, a little bit
of trade off in accuracy,

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:38.710
but typically not that much
if you do this correctly.

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:41.390
TensorFlow has built-in
support for quantization.

00:43:41.390 --> 00:43:42.600
So how does this work?

00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:46.010
Well, essentially, during
your training process,

00:43:46.010 --> 00:43:48.560
you're computing basically
the minimum and maximum value

00:43:48.560 --> 00:43:51.890
of these parameters,
say weights.

00:43:51.890 --> 00:43:55.270
You're storing that as
metadata along with the weight,

00:43:55.270 --> 00:43:57.530
and that allows you
basically to scale the weight

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:03.660
from wherever it occupies in its
range down to this 8-bit range.

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:05.660
So you don't just
store the weight,

00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:09.530
you store a minimum
and maximum value.

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.020
So it makes the graph a
little bit more complicated.

00:44:12.020 --> 00:44:15.560
But you know, it's much, much
faster to do these things with

00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:17.980
8-bit values than it is with--

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:19.332
There's no loss in performance.

00:44:19.332 --> 00:44:21.290
You get a little bit
extra of model complexity,

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:23.850
but you get a big
increase in performance.

00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:26.780
So it's relatively
straightforward to do this,

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:29.174
again using the
Graph Transform Tool.

00:44:29.174 --> 00:44:31.590
There's actually other another
tool in the TensorFlow code

00:44:31.590 --> 00:44:36.080
base that can be used for
quantization, called Quantized

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:37.760
Graph, I believe.

00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:40.070
We'd recommend that you
use this tool, frankly.

00:44:40.070 --> 00:44:43.250
I mean, they're both
roughly comparable,

00:44:43.250 --> 00:44:47.570
but Graph Transform Tool allows
you to incorporate several

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:50.030
of the other transforms
that are going

00:44:50.030 --> 00:44:52.160
to be useful when you're
doing this to a model

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:54.510
prior to serving.

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:57.350
TensorFlow Lite is
worth mentioning here.

00:44:57.350 --> 00:45:00.650
This was announced at the
recent TensorFlow Dev Summit,

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:04.070
and it's a framework
built into TensorFlow

00:45:04.070 --> 00:45:08.900
that basically does this
quantization process specified

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.490
for mobile devices or
customized for mobile devices.

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:16.250
So this just shows you a
graph of the relative gain

00:45:16.250 --> 00:45:19.350
in performance between
the TensorFlow model

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:23.330
and a TensorFlow Lite
quantized model running

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:25.491
on a mobile device.

00:45:25.491 --> 00:45:26.990
And I would encourage
you, if you're

00:45:26.990 --> 00:45:29.750
interested in running ML
on mobile, to check out

00:45:29.750 --> 00:45:32.270
this talk on the
TensorFlow Dev Summit.

00:45:32.270 --> 00:45:34.640
It's available on YouTube and
goes into much more detail

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:37.480
about TensorFlow Lite.

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:39.850
Finally, I'd to
talk about TensorRT.

00:45:39.850 --> 00:45:44.560
So our great partners
at NVIDIA have basically

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:46.430
been working on this
problem themselves--

00:45:46.430 --> 00:45:49.480
how do we optimize
inference given a model?

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:54.220
And they've also built some
quantization capabilities

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:55.100
into the hardware.

00:45:55.100 --> 00:45:57.730
So the V100 chip,
for example, includes

00:45:57.730 --> 00:46:01.360
what are called tensor
cores, which are essentially

00:46:01.360 --> 00:46:05.050
custom hardware components
or chip components that

00:46:05.050 --> 00:46:07.540
are optimized to
execute inference

00:46:07.540 --> 00:46:09.220
on these quantized values.

00:46:09.220 --> 00:46:11.050
And TensorRT is a
library that they

00:46:11.050 --> 00:46:14.530
provide that they've worked
with the TensorFlow team

00:46:14.530 --> 00:46:16.660
to integrate directly
into TensorFlow.

00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:20.170
And so it's relatively
straightforward,

00:46:20.170 --> 00:46:22.750
and you can get really,
really good performance

00:46:22.750 --> 00:46:25.180
boosts, particularly
on the chips

00:46:25.180 --> 00:46:27.730
like the V100 that have
specialized hardware

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:28.630
to support this.

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:32.620
So again, if you're
deploying inference on V100s,

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:36.700
you've got to use
TensorRT, or else you're

00:46:36.700 --> 00:46:40.000
giving up something that could
be on the order of 10x, maybe

00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:45.102
20x factors for performance.

00:46:45.102 --> 00:46:46.810
Finally, I just want
to talk a little bit

00:46:46.810 --> 00:46:49.240
about some
optimizations that you

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:50.920
can do to TensorFlow Serving.

00:46:50.920 --> 00:46:53.590
And then I think we'll have
maybe two minutes or so left

00:46:53.590 --> 00:46:54.790
for questions.

00:46:54.790 --> 00:46:57.260
I'm amazed that we got
through all of this.

00:46:57.260 --> 00:47:01.150
If we don't have time, if there
are more questions than we have

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:04.494
time for here, then
myself and Drew and Kevin

00:47:04.494 --> 00:47:06.410
are going to be out in
the hallway afterwards.

00:47:06.410 --> 00:47:09.659
So we're fine with you
guys approaching us,

00:47:09.659 --> 00:47:11.950
and we'll answer as many
questions as we can out there.

00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:14.380
All right, so let's talk
about optimizing TF Serving.

00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:15.820
Well, there's
really one thing you

00:47:15.820 --> 00:47:17.680
have to care about
when you're optimizing

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:22.180
TF Serving for scale,
and it's really important

00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:25.450
if you're going to do
TF Serving on GPUs.

00:47:25.450 --> 00:47:30.330
You won't actually get the
benefit of the GPU acceleration

00:47:30.330 --> 00:47:33.420
unless you do this, which
is to optimize the batching.

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:37.980
So basically, this
means instead of feeding

00:47:37.980 --> 00:47:41.850
a single predict example through
your TF Serving pipeline,

00:47:41.850 --> 00:47:44.430
you batch them
together in bunches,

00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:48.300
and you execute those all in
parallel through the pipeline.

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:51.520
And there is some
tuning of this process.

00:47:51.520 --> 00:47:54.120
There is a batching
guide, TensorFlow Serving

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:57.910
batching guide, that's in
the source for TF Serving.

00:47:57.910 --> 00:48:01.320
And I would encourage you, if
you're using TF Serving at all,

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:03.270
that you read this and
pay attention to it,

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:06.870
because it can make a
big difference in the way

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:10.030
that your TF Serving
pipeline performs.

00:48:10.030 --> 00:48:12.080
Well, that is it.

00:48:12.080 --> 00:48:14.130
Thank you very much.

